//
// loft#2062 — VECTORS WHOSE ELEMENTS NEED A CAST ARE NOT COMPARED (`@FR-E-Eq`, `@FR-C-Only`).  Owner
// ruling 2026-10-18: "if normally `as <type>` is needed it should not work" — a `character` and
// an `integer` element meet only through an explicit `as` (a plain `==` between the two scalars
// is admitted, the vector form is not), as do a `boolean`, a `text` and an enum.
//
// @falsified-at: 05d019607 — hand-measured, interpret and native: the file compiles and answers
//   by bytes.
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both element types.
// HOLDS: numeric element types compare by value (the sibling file) on both trees.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `==` compares the elements of a vector<character> with those of a vector<integer>

fn main() {
  a = ['a', 'b'];
  b = [97, 98];
  println("{a == b}");
}
